            {
              "id": "a2b31e8f-057e-3112-88e4-6506a9ce1d50",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\nThe `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name passed as input. If the specified environment variable is not found, the function returns a null pointer. If an attacker can control the environment, they can manipulate the return value of `getenv`, leading to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv` function in your code. If you need to use environment variables, consider safer alternatives that allow for better control and validation of the input. Always validate and sanitize the input to these functions to prevent potential security issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::getenv` function from the C++ standard library, which is considered safer:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nconst char* value = std::getenv(\"GGML_SYCL_NO_PINNED\");\nif (value != nullptr) {\n    // Use the value\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a0337ccf-03d0-3bd6-8205-ebae4173fa18",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path or mode is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to the `fopen` function. If you must use `fopen`, ensure that the file path and mode are properly validated and controlled. Avoid using user input directly in the file path or mode without proper sanitization and validation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fopen`, consider using a safer alternative such as `fopen_s` (in C11 and later), which includes error handling:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nerrno_t err;\nFILE* fptr;\nerr = fopen_s(&fptr, \"/proc/sys/kernel/numa_balancing\", \"r\");\nif (err != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdio.h` library.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4057cca0-b78a-38b8-887b-f1fbc545a1ad",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be risky and can lead to various security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards due to their inherent risks.\n\nIn the given code snippet `(i + 1) % 12 == 0`, there is no prohibited C function being used. However, if the variable `i` is not properly validated or controlled, it could potentially lead to an integer overflow vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this potential vulnerability, it is recommended to always validate and control the input values of variables. In this case, ensure that the value of `i` is within a safe range before performing the operation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix to ensure `i` is within a safe range:\n\n```cpp\nif (i >= 0 && i <= INT_MAX - 1) {\n    if ((i + 1) % 12 == 0) {\n        // Do something\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet does not require any specific library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE for Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8274c536-86ab-3f8f-812a-ee891bc59b59",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the `path` is not properly validated or sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to perform proper input validation and sanitization. In the case of `fopen`, it is important to ensure that the `path` variable is not under the control of an attacker, or that it is properly sanitized to prevent directory traversal attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the case of the provided code snippet, a possible fix would be to validate the `path` variable before using it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream spv;\nif (isValidPath(path)) {\n    spv.open(path.c_str(), std::ios::binary);\n    if (!spv) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid path\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `isValidPath` is a function that you would need to implement to validate the path. This could involve checking that the path does not contain any directory traversal sequences (like \"../\"), and that it points to a file within an expected directory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for the `std::ifstream` class.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a7da0e72-48cc-3d50-83a8-0085c48bd05f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, and other security issues. In the provided code, the function `strerror()` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded environment.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure and non-thread-safe C library functions. Instead, use their safer counterparts or functions provided by the C++ Standard Library. In the case of `strerror()`, consider using `strerror_r()` in POSIX systems or `strerror_s()` in Windows, which are thread-safe versions of `strerror()`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nvoid printError(const std::string& path, int errnum) {\n    char buf[1024];\n    if (strerror_r(errnum, buf, sizeof(buf)) == 0) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error reading SPIR-V file: \" << path << \" (\" << buf << \")\\n\";\n    } else {\n        std::cerr << \"Error reading SPIR-V file: \" << path << \" (Unknown error)\\n\";\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- iostream\n- string\n- cstring\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8edc860e-24de-3907-bcd4-b06559ee352e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to implement proper checks and controls to ensure that the function is used securely. For the `fopen` function, it is important to validate and control the file path that is passed to the function. This can prevent an attacker from opening arbitrary files or directories.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::string target_hpp = \"target.hpp\";\nstd::ofstream hdr(target_hpp);\nif (!hdr) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, the C++ `std::ofstream` class is used instead of the `fopen` function. This class provides a safer and more convenient interface for file I/O.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This header file is required for file I/O in C++.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
